---
description: Full-stack architecture rules for boundaries, contracts, and responsibility separation
alwaysApply: true
---

- Treat frontend and backend as separate modules with explicit contracts.
- Frontend must not depend on backend implementation details.
- Prefer typed request and response contracts.
- Keep business rules in services or use-cases, not in controllers, DTOs, or UI components.
- Keep validation close to boundaries:
  - frontend: form and schema validation
  - backend: DTO or schema validation
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Prefer feature-based organization on the frontend.
- Prefer module-based organization on the backend.
- Avoid duplicating validation logic when shared schemas or shared types can be used safely.
- Every new feature should define:
  - input contract
  - output contract
  - loading state
  - error state
  - empty state
  - authorization rules when applicable
- Avoid leaking infrastructure concerns into domain logic.
- Keep controllers thin.
- Keep pages thin.
- Prefer explicit dependencies over hidden coupling.
- Favor predictable folder structure over clever abstractions.
- When the challenge scope does not require a specific library, prefer the simplest maintainable implementation.
- Challenge requirements take precedence over personal stack preferences.